Nephrolithiasis (Kidney stones /Renal Colic)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Excruciating radiating flank pain, Extreme physical restlessness, Hyperdense calculus on CT KUB

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-025
Reason for Visit: Excruciating, unremitting flank pain and intractable vomiting requiring immediate parenteral analgesia and antiemetics.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient in extreme agony, constantly pacing with severe flank pain radiating to the groin, and unable to tolerate oral fluids due to profuse vomiting. Urgent evaluation was strictly necessary to exclude a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm and to administer immediate intramuscular analgesia to manage the severe autonomic distress before the next routine clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am in absolute agony, the pain in my side is worse than anything I have ever felt, and I cannot stop throwing up.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden onset of excruciating, agonizing unilateral flank pain that awoke them from sleep two hours ago. The pain rapidly escalated, radiating anteriorly towards the ipsilateral groin and testicle. They exhibit extreme physical restlessness, forcing them to constantly writhe and pace the room in an attempt to find a comfortable position, which is impossible. They describe severe nausea and recurrent profuse vomiting, rendering them completely unable to tolerate oral fluids. They noted a single episode of macroscopic haematuria just prior to the pain peaking. They deny any fever or dysuria prior to this acute event.
Functional Status: Fully ambulatory but entirely incapacitated by agonizing pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe dehydration risk due to intractable vomiting; extreme acute pain causing severe distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, pale, tachycardic, continuously writhing and pacing the examination room, unable to sit still for vital signs.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Soft, completely non-tender anteriorly. Complete absence of generalized peritonitis, pulsatile mass, or abdominal rigidity. Back: Severe unilateral costovertebral angle tenderness upon percussion on the affected side. Genitourinary: Point-of-care urinalysis reveals marked microscopic haematuria but is completely negative for nitrites and leukocytes. The physical presentation is dominated by pallor, diaphoresis, and tachycardia secondary to the extreme pain. Expected investigation results would reveal a hyperdense radiopaque calculus visualized within the urinary tract on a non-contrast CT KUB.
Environmental Observations: Multiple emesis basins utilized; patient has discarded heavy clothing due to profound diaphoresis.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken as patient immediately vomited their attempt at oral paracetamol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Nephrolithiasis (Renal Colic) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ruptured Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a pulsatile abdominal mass, absence of profound hypotension/shock, and the presence of haematuria.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's constant pacing (peritonitic patients lie perfectly still), absence of right lower quadrant rigidity, and absence of fever.

Acute Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely afebrile presentation and lack of infectious markers on the urinalysis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark features of acute renal colic: sudden, agonizing flank-to-groin pain accompanied by extreme physical restlessness and intractable vomiting. The haematuria and unilateral renal angle pain confirm the urological origin. The severity of the pain generates a massive sympathetic response (tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis). An urgent physical assessment was absolutely required to definitively exclude life-threatening vascular catastrophes (like a leaking AAA) and to administer immediate, powerful parenteral analgesics and antiemetics, as oral medications were completely futile. Leaving this patient in unmitigated agony until normal clinic hours would constitute a severe failure of medical care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg stat for potent, direct prostaglandin-inhibiting analgesia. Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg to arrest the intractable vomiting. Ordered an urgent non-contrast CT KUB and formal urine microscopy for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To assist in the eventual passage of the stone once the vomiting has ceased, the patient is instructed to consume fluids using a dedicated 1-liter water bottle with time markers. By meeting the specific hourly volume goals marked on the bottle, the patient achieves a continuous high urine flow rate. The visible progress of emptying the bottle provides positive reinforcement to maintain the hydration necessary to flush the calculus.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urine strainer provided to catch the calculus for chemical analysis.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to review the CT KUB imaging, assess pain control, and check renal function bloods.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required if the patient develops a fever with uncontrollable shivering, if the pain becomes completely unresponsive to the administered injection, or if they completely stop passing urine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical picture is classic for a kidney stone, but a CT scan is required to confirm the exact size and location to ensure it can pass naturally.
Clinical Course: The injection will provide significant pain relief within 30 minutes. The pain may return in waves as the stone moves down the ureter over the next few days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a fever and start shaking, or if the pain returns to an agonizing level that you cannot tolerate, you must go directly to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A hard stone has blocked the narrow tube draining your kidney. Your kidney is forcefully squeezing to push it out, causing the agonizing pain and vomiting. The anti-inflammatory injection I gave you specifically stops those severe spasms and will ease the pain shortly.
Actionable Steps: Once the nausea settles, drink large amounts of water using the marked bottle to flush the stone out. Strain all your urine through the provided sieve. Attend the urgent CT scan tomorrow.

